ject + <b>can/ may + verb in base form </b>+ complement<br><br></center>  Example: <br><i> You <b>can use</b> ASP.NET to develop web applications. <br><br></i>  In the case, the modal verb may refer to something that not always happens or has less possibilities of being true. <br><br>  Example: <br><i>  You <b>may develop</b> a lot of projects at the same time when using ASP.NET."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Visual Studio can create solution files once you start working on a website."}, {"type": "example", "content": "These solution files may have many projects at the same time. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Simple Past Tense Questions to Confirm"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">past simple tense</span></span> to describe actions that happened in a specific time in the past. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use simple questions in the past tense to check understanding or to ask for confirmation about specific information.  <br><br> When you ask questions, the structure is different from the affirmative and negative forms. You don't have to use the past form of the verb; you use the verb in infinitive. In addition, you need to include the auxiliary did at the beginning of the question. <br><br>  To make questions, you use the following structure:<br><br><center>  <b>Did </b>+ subject + <b>verb in base form</b> + complement +?</center><br><br>  Examples:<br><i> <b>Did </b>you <b>compile</b> a source file before executing an application? <br> <b>Did </b>he <b>say </b> that ASP.NET has solution modules? <br> <b>Did </b> they <b>know</b> that solutions can have more than one project?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Did he continue talking about ASP.NET?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Did you know that all those application items constitute a solution?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Did he bring up some points on how to run a project 