 "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob08re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "template_override": 1, "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 5, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le03ob08re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<b>Hannah:</b> I couldn't assist to Mr. Kapoor's class this morning.  Did he talk about ASP.NET?<br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Yeah, he explained some details about applications. <br>\n<b>Hannah:</b> Did he mention the components? <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Yes. He said that an ASP.NET application can have different items such as web content files, source files, assemblies, or user controls. <br>\n<b>Hannah:</b> Did he list the extensions? <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Yes, you'd better take notes. So, .aspx is for web content files, .cs for source files, .dll or .exe for assemblies and .ascx for user controls. <br>"}, {"time": "43500", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<b>Hannah:</b> Thanks. Did you know that all those application items constitute a solution?<br>\n<b>Tom:</b> That's right, Visual Studio can create solution files once you start working on a website. These solution files may have many projects at the same time.  <br>\n<b>Hannah:</b> I see. So, did Mr. Kapoor mean that a solution includes other solutions? <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Not at all! A solution may consist of one or many more projects, but solutions can't have other solutions. <br>\n<b>Hannah:</b> I got it. What did he explain about projects? <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Well, he said that projects are like folders to save information such as, images, content or source files, while the extensions of application projects can be .cjproj or .vbproj. <br>\n<b>Hannah:</b> But, I think that executable files or dynamic link libraries can also store the information of a project. <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Absolutely!  .exe and .dll files store project data. They save files such as page, user control, web service, master page, site map, and website configuration files. <br>"}, {"time": "117500", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<b>Hannah:</b> Did he bring up some points on how to run a project application? <br>\n<b>Tom:</b> Yes, Mr. Kapoor gave some instructions to execute an application. First, you should open your project in VB.net and then you go to properties. Once there, you select \u201cLocal ISS server\u201d, and you select start but without debugging."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le03ob08re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}